WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.375
Picture a restaurant kitchen.

2
00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:08.996
Waiters write each order on a ticket and clip it to a metal bar called the rail.

3
00:00:08.996 --> 00:00:11.595
Cooks take the oldest ticket off and cook it.

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:18.555
In a program, each waiter and each cook is a thread, a line of work that runs side by side with the others.

5
00:00:18.555 --> 00:00:24.805
The rail has one key, the lock from the last lesson: only the thread holding it may touch the rail.

6
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:26.636
Now the rail is empty.

7
00:00:26.636 --> 00:00:28.311
How should a cook wait?

8
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:30.806
The quick answer is to keep asking.

9
00:00:30.806 --> 00:00:31.650
Anything yet?

10
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:32.494
Anything yet?

11
00:00:32.494 --> 00:00:37.819
Like a kid in the back seat asking if we're there yet, millions of times a second.

12
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:39.602
That's called a busy wait.

13
00:00:39.602 --> 00:00:43.524
It works, but it keeps a whole processor core busy doing nothing.

14
00:00:43.524 --> 00:00:48.592
In a test, waiting one second this way burned about a full second of processor time.

15
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.863
What we want is a cook that sleeps until the rail changes, and a waiter who wakes it up.

16
00:00:54.067 --> 00:00:56.537
Java's kitchen has three more things.

17
00:00:56.537 --> 00:00:59.875
A line by the rail, for threads that want the key.

18
00:00:59.875 --> 00:01:01.210
A bench at the back.

19
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:01.944
And a bell.

20
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:06.115
The cook takes the key and finds the rail empty, so it calls wait.

21
00:01:06.115 --> 00:01:09.464
That hangs the key back, and sits the cook on the bench.

22
00:01:09.464 --> 00:01:13.472
Later, when it's woken, it must get the key back before it goes on.

23
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.694
A waiter takes the key, clips a ticket on, and calls notifyAll.

24
00:01:17.694 --> 00:01:18.962
That rings the bell.

25
00:01:18.962 --> 00:01:22.829
Everyone on the bench gets up and joins the line for the key.

26
00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.872
The waiter hangs the key back.

27
00:01:24.872 --> 00:01:27.813
The cook gets it, sees the ticket, and takes it.

28
00:01:27.813 --> 00:01:30.755
On the bench, the cook used no processor at all.

29
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:36.842
With one cook, an if seems enough: if the rail is empty, wait, then take a ticket.

30
00:01:36.842 --> 00:01:38.141
It passes every test.

31
00:01:38.141 --> 00:01:40.617
So here's the question we're shy to ask.

32
00:01:40.617 --> 00:01:43.154
Why does everyone insist on a while loop?

33
00:01:43.154 --> 00:01:46.435
If the bell woke me, the ticket must be there, right?

34
00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:48.351
Let's hire a second cook.

35
00:01:48.351 --> 00:01:50.912
Both find the rail empty and sit down.

36
00:01:50.912 --> 00:01:54.146
A waiter clips one ticket on and rings the bell.

37
00:01:54.146 --> 00:01:55.696
What happens to cook 2?

38
00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.077
Both cooks get up and join the line.

39
00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:04.224
Cook 1 gets the key first and takes the ticket.

40
00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:05.898
Then cook 2 gets the key.

41
00:02:05.898 --> 00:02:08.577
Its if ran long ago, before it sat down.

42
00:02:08.577 --> 00:02:10.519
So it grabs at an empty rail.

43
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:12.261
The demo printed this.

44
00:02:12.261 --> 00:02:18.648
Cook 2 failed with NoSuchElementException, Java's complaint about taking from an empty list.

45
00:02:18.648 --> 00:02:20.940
Who wins changes from run to run.

46
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.259
The ending doesn't.

47
00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:25.634
So the wrong idea was this: woken means it's mine.

48
00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:28.485
But the bell never says this ticket is yours.

49
00:02:28.485 --> 00:02:30.956
It says something changed, go and look.

50
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:33.851
Change if to while, and cook 2 looks again.

51
00:02:33.851 --> 00:02:38.284
The rail is empty, so it sits back down, and waits for the next ticket.

52
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:40.420
There's a stranger reason, too.

53
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.198
Java lets a thread get up from the bench with no bell at all.

54
00:02:44.198 --> 00:02:46.055
It's called a spurious wakeup.

55
00:02:46.055 --> 00:02:48.099
Java's cooks sometimes sleepwalk.

56
00:02:48.099 --> 00:02:50.267
So even a lone cook needs the loop.

57
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.358
Now the names.

58
00:02:51.358 --> 00:02:54.973
The line is the entry set, and its threads show as BLOCKED.

59
00:02:54.973 --> 00:02:58.589
The bench is the wait set, and its threads show as WAITING.

60
00:02:58.589 --> 00:03:02.879
And hold the key, check, wait until it's true: that's a guarded block.

61
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:06.412
The rule: being woken doesn't mean the state is right.

62
00:03:06.412 --> 00:03:08.191
Check again, in a while loop.

63
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.449
One more change.

64
00:03:09.449 --> 00:03:12.006
A real rail holds only so many tickets.

65
00:03:12.006 --> 00:03:14.498
So when it's full, a waiter waits too.

66
00:03:14.498 --> 00:03:16.662
At last, the waiters get to wait.

67
00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:23.153
Now two kinds of threads share one bench: cooks waiting for a ticket, and waiters waiting for room.

68
00:03:23.367 --> 00:03:27.371
So why ring for everyone, when one ticket needs one cook?

69
00:03:27.371 --> 00:03:30.532
Java has notify, which wakes just one thread.

70
00:03:30.532 --> 00:03:31.445
Let's try it.

71
00:03:31.445 --> 00:03:36.503
Room for one ticket, two waiters, two cooks, and every bell is a notify.

72
00:03:36.503 --> 00:03:37.416
What happens?

73
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.608
Both cooks find the rail empty and sit down.

74
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:47.424
Waiter 1 clips a ticket and wakes one thread, cook 1.

75
00:03:47.633 --> 00:03:52.312
Before cook 1 gets the key, both waiters find the rail full and sit down.

76
00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:55.514
Cook 1 takes the ticket and wakes one thread.

77
00:03:55.514 --> 00:03:57.368
It's cook 2, the wrong kind.

78
00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.506
Cook 2 finds no ticket and sits back down.

79
00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:04.035
Then cook 1 comes back for more, and sits down too.

80
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:07.691
Now all four threads sit on the bench, waiting for a bell.

81
00:04:07.691 --> 00:04:10.701
And everyone who could ring it is on the bench too.

82
00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:17.523
In the demo, the kitchen stalled after 8 orders, with every thread WAITING.

83
00:04:17.523 --> 00:04:25.989
With notifyAll, the same four threads cooked 136,000 to 139,000 orders in five seconds, and never stalled.

84
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:31.799
notifyAll wakes everyone, and each while loop sends back the threads that can't go on.

85
00:04:31.799 --> 00:04:38.309
notify is safe only when every sleeper waits for the same thing, and one change lets just one go on.

86
00:04:39.033 --> 00:04:42.077
In code, Java on the left and Kotlin on the right.

87
00:04:42.077 --> 00:04:46.641
Hold the key, wait in a while loop, take the ticket, and ring for everyone.

88
00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:48.921
So, why the while loop?

89
00:04:48.921 --> 00:04:50.946
Because the bell is only a hint.

90
00:04:50.946 --> 00:04:52.527
Woken doesn't mean ready.

91
00:04:52.527 --> 00:04:57.840
Change the rail before you ring, ring for everyone, and check again in a while loop.

92
00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:58.876
Quick check.

93
00:04:58.876 --> 00:05:02.384
One cook, one waiter, and the cook waits with an if.

94
00:05:02.384 --> 00:05:03.868
Can it still go wrong?
